## Why This Is Asked
Tests beginner-level understanding of end-to-end performance, tail latency, and backpressure in a real-time chat system, using concrete metrics and simple tooling.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency and tail latency targets
- Throughput vs latency under WebSocket load
- Backpressure indicators (gateway queue depth, dropped websockets)
- Workload modeling for chat apps (channels, message rate, payload size)
- Basic failure scenarios (network jitter, partial drops)

## Code Example
```javascript
import { check } from 'k6';
import ws from 'k6/ws';

export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://chat.example.test/ws';
  const params = { headers: { 'User-Agent': 'perf-test' } };

  const res = ws.connect(url, params, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function open() {
      // send a burst of messages across channels
      // placeholder for batch-send logic
    });
    socket.on('message', function (msg) {
      // simple ack handling
      check(msg, { 'ack received': (m) => m != null });
    });
  });

  check(res, { 'connected': (r) => r && r.status === 101 });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust tests to reflect production traffic patterns across time zones and peak windows?
- What instrumentation would you add to isolate client-side vs server-side latency contributions?

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding end-to-end performance in a service mesh with multiple microservices and chaos scenarios is critical for real-world reliability.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end tail latency across services
- Workload modeling (Poisson bursts, ramp stages)
- Backpressure and circuit breaking
- Observability (OpenTelemetry, metrics, traces)
- Chaos testing and fault injection in Istio

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = { stages: [ { duration: '5m', target: 20000 } ] };
export default function () {
  const r = http.post('https://service/booking', JSON.stringify({ rider:'r1', pickup:'A', dropoff:'B' }), { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }});
  check(r, {'status is 200': (r)=> r.status === 200});
  sleep(0.5);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for geo-distributed deployments?
- What thresholds would you set for p95/p99 under varied network conditions?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical ability to design end-to-end perf tests for a streaming telemetry pipeline, focusing on tail latency, backpressure, autoscaling, and cross-service bottlenecks.

## Key Concepts

- Tail latency under burst traffic
- Workload modeling (phased ramp, duration)
- End-to-end instrumentation (ingestion, cache, DB)
- Backpressure and autoscaling triggers
- Bottleneck triage across services and storage

## Code Example

```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 1000 },
    { duration: '8m', target: 0 }
  ]
};

export default function () {
  const payload = JSON.stringify({ deviceId: __VU, payload: 'x'.repeat(256) });
  const res = http.post('https://ingest.example/api/telemetry', payload, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
  });
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you isolate whether ingestion, cache, or DB is the bottleneck?
- What metrics and dashboards would you expose to identify autoscaling efficacy?

## Why This Is Asked

